{
  "meta": {
    "level": "B2",
    "section": "Grammar",
    "number": 8,
    "total_in_level": 10,
    "title": "Emphasis, Word Order and Suffixes",
    "subtitle": "clefts, focus and repeated pronouns, exclamations, diminutives, augmentatives and -ísimo",
    "footer": "Spanish Learning Program · B2 · Grammar · Emphasis, word order, suffixes",
    "next": "Next: B2 · PDF 9 — Word formation and register"
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "tip",
      "title": "After this PDF you can",
      "bullets": [
        "emphasise one part of a sentence (Fue Ana quien llamó, Lo que quiero es ...)",
        "use word order for focus and repeated pronouns (El libro lo compré ayer)",
        "use intensifiers and exclamations (qué ... más, ni siquiera, vaya)",
        "use diminutives, augmentatives and -ísimo correctly"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "1. Cleft sentences (focus)"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "style": "plain",
      "widths": [
        5.0,
        6.2,
        5.8
      ],
      "head": [
        "Structure",
        "Example",
        "English"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "ser + focus + que",
          "Fue ayer cuando lo vi.",
          "It was yesterday that I saw him."
        ],
        [
          "ser + person + quien / el que",
          "Fue Ana quien llamó.",
          "It was Ana who called."
        ],
        [
          "ser + place + donde",
          "Es en Madrid donde vivo.",
          "It is in Madrid where I live."
        ],
        [
          "ser + manner + como",
          "Así es como se hace.",
          "This is how it is done."
        ],
        [
          "lo que + verb + ser + focus",
          "Lo que quiero es descansar.",
          "What I want is to rest."
        ],
        [
          "lo que pasa es que",
          "Lo que pasa es que no tengo tiempo.",
          "The thing is that I have no time."
        ],
        [
          "es que (excuse)",
          "¿Por qué no vienes? Es que estoy cansado.",
          "Why do you not come? It is that I am tired."
        ],
        [
          "el que / la que + verb + ser",
          "La que llamó fue tu madre.",
          "The one who called was your mother."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "warn",
      "title": "Agreement in clefts",
      "text": "The relative agrees with the focus: <i>Fueron mis padres quienes pagaron.</i> <i>Somos nosotros los que decidimos.</i> The verb ser agrees with the focus too: <i>Son ellas las que mandan.</i>"
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "2. Word order and repeated pronouns"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Spanish word order is flexible. The most important (new) information often goes <b>last</b>; known information goes first."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "style": "plain",
      "widths": [
        6.0,
        6.0,
        5.0
      ],
      "head": [
        "Neutral",
        "Marked (focus)",
        "Effect"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Compré el libro ayer.",
          "El libro lo compré ayer.",
          "El libro: topic, repeated by lo"
        ],
        [
          "Vi a Ana ayer.",
          "A Ana la vi ayer.",
          "topic fronted"
        ],
        [
          "Llegó un paquete.",
          "Un paquete llegó. / Llegó un paquete.",
          "new info goes last"
        ],
        [
          "Ana habla español.",
          "Español habla Ana.",
          "contrast: Spanish, not French"
        ],
        [
          "Me gusta esto.",
          "Esto sí que me gusta.",
          "emphasis with sí que"
        ],
        [
          "No me gusta nada.",
          "Nada me gusta.",
          "negative fronted"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "bullets",
      "items": [
        "When the <b>object comes first</b>, repeat it with a pronoun: <i>A Pedro lo conozco. Los libros los dejé en casa.</i>",
        "<b>Subject after the verb</b> is normal for new information: <i>Ha llamado tu madre. Vino mucha gente.</i>",
        "After a question word the subject normally follows the verb: <i>¿Qué dijo Ana?</i> (not <i>¿Qué Ana dijo?</i>)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "3. Intensifiers and exclamations"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "style": "plain",
      "widths": [
        5.0,
        6.2,
        5.8
      ],
      "head": [
        "Structure",
        "Example",
        "English"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "¡Qué + adj. / noun!",
          "¡Qué bonito! ¡Qué suerte!",
          "How beautiful! What luck!"
        ],
        [
          "¡Qué + noun + más / tan + adj.!",
          "¡Qué día más / tan bonito!",
          "What a beautiful day!"
        ],
        [
          "¡Cuánto / cuántos!",
          "¡Cuánto has crecido!",
          "How you have grown!"
        ],
        [
          "¡Vaya + noun!",
          "¡Vaya sorpresa!",
          "What a surprise!"
        ],
        [
          "¡Mira que + verb!",
          "¡Mira que eres tonto!",
          "You really are silly!"
        ],
        [
          "ni siquiera",
          "Ni siquiera me llamó.",
          "He did not even call me."
        ],
        [
          "ni ... ni ...",
          "No tengo ni tiempo ni dinero.",
          "I have neither time nor money."
        ],
        [
          "sí que / no ... sino",
          "No es caro, sino barato.",
          "It is not expensive but cheap."
        ],
        [
          "tan ... que",
          "Es tan alto que no cabe.",
          "He is so tall that he does not fit."
        ],
        [
          "tanto ... que",
          "Llovió tanto que se inundó.",
          "It rained so much that it flooded."
        ],
        [
          "apenas, recién",
          "Apenas lo conozco.",
          "I hardly know him."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "spain",
      "title": "Vaya, hombre, mira, anda",
      "text": "Spain loves colloquial interjections: <b>¡Vaya! ¡Anda! ¡Venga! ¡Hombre! ¡Mira!</b> are used constantly: <i>¡Venga, vámonos! ¡Anda ya! (No way!) ¡Hombre, qué sorpresa!</i>"
    },
    {
      "type": "pagebreak"
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "4. Diminutives"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Diminutives show small size, affection, politeness or sometimes irony. In Spain <b>-ito / -ita</b> and <b>-illo / -illa</b> dominate."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "style": "plain",
      "widths": [
        4.4,
        4.4,
        4.2,
        4.0
      ],
      "head": [
        "Ending",
        "Rule",
        "Example",
        "English"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "-ito / -ita",
          "default",
          "casa: casita; perro: perrito",
          "little house; puppy"
        ],
        [
          "-cito / -cita",
          "after -n, -r, -e",
          "pan: pancito; café: cafecito",
          "bread roll; small coffee"
        ],
        [
          "-ecito / -ecita",
          "after one-syllable words",
          "flor: florecita; luz: lucecita",
          "little flower; small light"
        ],
        [
          "-illo / -illa",
          "default (Spain)",
          "ventana: ventanilla; chico: chiquillo",
          "small window; kid"
        ],
        [
          "-ico / -ica",
          "Aragón, Murcia",
          "gato: gatico",
          "little cat"
        ],
        [
          "c to qu / g to gu",
          "spelling",
          "poco: poquito; amigo: amiguito",
          "a little; little friend"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "bullets",
      "items": [
        "<b>Adverbs and adjectives:</b> <i>cerquita, despacito, rapidito, chiquitito, poquito</i>.",
        "<b>Politeness:</b> <i>Un momentito, por favor. ¿Me da un cafetito?</i> softens a request.",
        "<b>Irony:</b> <i>¡Menudo problemita!</i> (what a \"little\" problem) can mean a big problem."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "5. Augmentatives and pejoratives"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "style": "plain",
      "widths": [
        3.4,
        6.2,
        7.4
      ],
      "head": [
        "Ending",
        "Example",
        "Meaning"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "-ón / -ona",
          "grande: grandote; hombre: hombretón",
          "bigger, stronger or ugly"
        ],
        [
          "-azo / -aza",
          "coche: cochazo; golpe: golpazo; manos: manazas",
          "big, great or a blow"
        ],
        [
          "-ote / -ota",
          "grande: grandote; palabra: palabrota",
          "big, rude"
        ],
        [
          "-ucho / -ucha",
          "casa: casucha; flaco: flacucho",
          "poor, ugly (pejorative)"
        ],
        [
          "-aco / -aca",
          "pájaro: pajarraco; libro: libraco",
          "pejorative"
        ],
        [
          "-ísimo / -ísima",
          "bueno: buenísimo; rico: riquísimo",
          "very (superlative)"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "-ísimo (absolute superlative)"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "style": "plain",
      "widths": [
        4.4,
        4.4,
        4.2,
        4.0
      ],
      "head": [
        "Adjective",
        "Superlative",
        "Adjective",
        "Superlative"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "bueno",
          "buenísimo",
          "difícil",
          "dificilísimo"
        ],
        [
          "rico",
